## Cross-narrator notes

Things that hold across all narrators (author-level voice, keep):

- [Comma-splice as default sentence-joiner.]
- [Engineering precision in description of magic / machinery.]
- [Casual profanity (with character-varied frequency).]
- [Whatever the source author always does.]

Things that change between narrators (character-voice, hold separate):

- [Fragment frequency: narrator A high, narrator B low.]
- [Self-deprecation patterns.]
- [Editorialising distance.]
- [Magic-system register: user vs designer vs learner.]

If any rewrite scene has more than one POV in play (only specific chapters alternate), the writer's brief must specify which voice is on the page and the reviewer must enforce. Cross-POV bleed is the single most common reviewer-flag risk for any multi-narrator novel.
